# Security headers, applied by Cloudflare Pages.
#
# This file is copied verbatim from public/ into out/ at build time. Cloudflare
# reads it from the root of the deployed output. There is no build step for it,
# so a typo fails quietly — after deploying, check the response headers on the
# live site rather than assuming.
#
# Comments must start at column 0. An indented "#" is parsed as a header name,
# not a comment, which is why the notes below sit between blocks instead of
# inside them.
#
#
# One honest limitation up front: script-src needs 'unsafe-inline'. Next.js
# emits inline bootstrap and hydration scripts, and a static export has no
# server to mint a per-request nonce. Hashes would work but change on every
# build, so a stale _headers file would take the whole app down.
#
# That costs less than it sounds, because the directive actually defending the
# vault is connect-src. Even if a script were injected, it could not send the
# decrypted contents anywhere — the browser refuses connections to any host not
# named here. Exfiltration is the thing worth blocking; execution is secondary.
#
# connect-src names one project and nothing else. It was *.supabase.co while the
# project did not exist yet, which would have allowed an injected script to post
# the decrypted vault to any Supabase project it liked - a free account and a
# wildcard is a low bar. If the project ref ever changes, this line has to change
# with it, and the symptom of forgetting is every request failing with a CSP
# violation in the console rather than anything subtle.
#
# COEP is deliberately absent. It would add nothing here — no SharedArrayBuffer,
# no cross-origin isolation requirement — and credentialless mode breaks fetches
# of encrypted photos from Supabase Storage unless their CORP headers line up.
# A header that silently stops photos loading is worse than no header.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' blob: data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://mhoxsqbcndzeoexlqzdl.supabase.co wss://mhoxsqbcndzeoexlqzdl.supabase.co; media-src 'self' blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), camera=(self), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# Notes on the block above:
#
#   X-Frame-Options   - frame-ancestors already covers this; kept for browsers
#                       old enough to ignore CSP framing directives.
#   HSTS              - two years, subdomains, preload-eligible. Without it one
#                       http:// request on a cafe network strips TLS.
#   Referrer-Policy   - a vault URL should not appear in anyone else's logs.
#   Permissions-Policy- camera=(self) stays because scanning items needs it.
#                       Geolocation and microphone are refused outright.
#
#
# Caching. The bundles under /_next/static are content-hashed and safe to keep
# forever. The HTML that references them is not: a cached page after a key
# rotation or a security fix is a page still running the old rules.

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-store

/
  Cache-Control: no-store

/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
